-- crop types

Altitude plays an important role in the distribution of wheat production through its influence on rainfall, temperature, and diseases. In Arsi, Bale and Shewa regions, the soil, moisture and disease conditions within the range of 1900-2300 m altitude zones are favorable for the production of early and intermediate maturing varieties of bread wheat. This is estimated to comprise 25% of the total wheat production area, while the remaining 75% falls in the 2300-2700 m altitude zone. There, early, intermediate and late varieties are grown. Soil types used for wheat production vary from well-drained fertile soils to waterlogged heavy vertisols.

So far, different varieties of bread wheat varieties have been released and/ or registered to satisfy the growing production demand of the farmers in the country', 102, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('broccoli', 'croptype-705766473095', 'fruit-and-vegetables', 'Broccoli', NULL, 'Brassica oleracea', NULL, 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/broccoli.jpg', NULL, 4, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('buck-wheat', 'croptype-432965158580', 'cereal', 'Buck wheat', NULL, 'Fagopyrum esculentum Moench', 'DZARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Buck wheat.jpg', 'Buck wheat (Fagopyrum esculentum Moench) is a recent introduction where its cultivation and use is new to Ethiopia. It is relatively low yielder compared to other grain crops, however, its unique characters such as its ability to perform better relatively on poor soils and its quick maturity, suiting areas with very short rains and as a catch crop where other crops failed or planting is delayed, makes it important.

Though the grain is used in ways similar to cereals, buckwheat is a non-cereal, broadleaved herbaceous plant that flowers prolifically over a period of several weeks belonging to plygonaceae family.

The grain is a source of human food where the flour and /or groats could be used for making different recipes either solely or mixed with other cereal grains; it is also a good feed source to poultry and livestock. The extended nectar supply, for a month or more, for bees makes buckwheat popular among beekeepers. Besides, it is also an important smother and green manure crop.', 1, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('cabbage', 'croptype-776586950749', 'fruit-and-vegetables', 'Cabbage', NULL, 'Brassica oleracea', 'MARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/cabbage.jpg', 'Cabbage (Brassica oleracea, capitata group) has short stem and a globular head of tightly overlapping green to purplish leaves. In Ethiopia, the total area covered by head cabbage and Ethiopian cabbage in 2020/21 Meher cropping season was 51,114.46 ha and the total production during this season was about 4,891,287.23 quintals.', 21, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('cacao', 'croptype-179012347549', 'industrial-crops', 'Cacao', NULL, 'Theobroma cacao', 'TNSRC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/cacao.jpg', NULL, 1, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('camelina', 'croptype-169132888124', 'industrial-crops', 'Camelina', NULL, 'Camelina sativa', 'HARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/camelina.jfif', 'Camelina (Camelina sativa) is an oleaginous plant from the Cruciferae family, which has been domesticated and extensively used in Europe and America for several thousand years. The crop has multitude of uses among which its edible oil being the major one. The seed oil of Camelina contains an exceptional amount (up to 45%) of omega-3 fatty acids, as well as a unique antioxidant complex making the oil very stable and resistant to heat and rancidity. Unlike any other omega-3 oil, camelina oil is perfectly suitable for use not only as a well balanced omega-3 supplement, but also as a health promoting everyday cooking oil.', 2, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('cardamom', 'croptype-042097140836', 'spices-condiments-medicinal-aromatic', 'Cardamom', NULL, 'Elettaria cardamomum', 'TNSRC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Cardamom.jpg', NULL, 1, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('carrot', 'croptype-563334855049', 'fruit-and-vegetables', 'Carrot', '{"am": "ካሮት"}'::jsonb, 'Daucus carota L.', 'Haramaya University', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/carrot.jpg', 'Carrot (Daucus corota L.) belongs to the family Apiaceae. It is rich in carotene (pro-vitamin A).

In Ethiopia, the area covered by carrot in 2020/21 Meher cropping season is about 3,717.22 ha and the total production is estimated to be 196,387.07 quintals.', 2, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('cassava', 'croptype-210865675426', 'roots-and-tubers', 'Cassava', NULL, 'Manihot esculenta', 'HawARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/casava.jpg', 'Cassava is widely cultivated in many parts of the world. It is a perennial shrub, with latex in all its parts, which produces enlarged tuberous root. Cassava root is eaten as a vegetable, and as it toxic raw it is eaten after cooking.', 6, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('castor', 'croptype-078847480249', 'industrial-crops', 'Castor', '{"am": "ጉሎ"}'::jsonb, 'Ricinus communis L.', 'MARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Castor.jpg', 'Castor is a flowering plant in the spurge family, Euphorbiaceae. It is indigenous to the southeastern Mediterranean Basin, Eastern Africa, and India. Castor seed is the source of castor oil, which has a wide variety of uses. The seeds contain between 40 and 60% oil that is rich in triglycerides, mainly ricinolein. The seed contain ricin, a toxin, which is also present in lower concentrations through the plant.', 2, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('cauliflower', 'croptype-069959015564', 'fruit-and-vegetables', 'Cauliflower', NULL, 'Brassica oleracea', 'HARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/cauliflower.jpg', NULL, 5, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('chamomile', 'croptype-383110700217', 'spices-condiments-medicinal-aromatic', 'Chamomile', '{"am": "ከሞሜላ"}'::jsonb, 'Matricaria chamomilla L.', 'DZARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Chamomile.jpg', NULL, 2, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('chickpea', 'croptype-731005222916', 'food-legume', 'Chickpea', '{"am": "ሽምብራ"}'::jsonb, 'Cicer arietinum', 'DZARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/chickpea.jpg', 'Chickpea is one of the major highland pulse crop widely grown in the highland and semi-highland regions of Ethiopia mainly on clay soil and fixes atmospheric nitrogen in soils and thus improves soil febrility and saves fertilizer costs in subsequent crops. In 2020/21 cropping season, 220,719.20 hectares of land was covered with chickpea and the production was estimated at about 4,573,193.70 quintals. Because of its multiple importance, the crop is widely produced by the Ethiopian farmers. Chickpea is widely used for food for its high protein content. Apart from this, because of its ability to fix nitrogen it is used in crop rotation with the nationally important cereal crops like wheat, tef and barley.', 34, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('citronella-grass', 'croptype-089152903238', 'spices-condiments-medicinal-aromatic', 'Citronella grass', '{"am": "የናርዶስ ሳር"}'::jsonb, 'Cymbopogon winteranius', 'WGARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Citronella grass.jpg', NULL, 1, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('coffee', 'croptype-871314011499', 'stimulant-crops', 'Coffee', '{"am": "ቡና"}'::jsonb, 'Coffea arabica L.', 'JARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/coffee.webp', NULL, 43, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('common-bean', 'croptype-774077003541', 'food-legume', 'Common bean', NULL, 'Phaseolus vulgaris L.', 'Jimma Agricultural Research Cent', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/commonbean.jpg', NULL, 5, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('coriander', 'croptype-367727453468', 'spices-condiments-medicinal-aromatic', 'Coriander', '{"am": "ድምብላል"}'::jsonb, 'Coriandrum sativum L.', 'DZARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/coriander.jpg', NULL, 7, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('cotton', 'croptype-306057170909', 'industrial-crops', 'Cotton', '{"am": "ጥጥ"}'::jsonb, 'Gossypium hirsutum', 'WARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/cotton-field.webp', 'Cotton is one of the major industrial and cash crops in Ethiopia, mainly supporting the textile and garment industry. The crop is predominantly grown in the country’s lowland irrigated and rain-fed areas with warm climates. The major cotton-growing areas include the Awash Valley (Afar and Oromia), Humera and Metema in Amhara, Gambella, Benishangul-Gumuz, and parts of South Ethiopia. The major potential production basins are Awash, Omo-Ghibe, Wabi-Shebelle, Baro-Akobo, Blue Nile, and Tekeze river basins. Recent studies indicate that Ethiopia cultivates only a small portion of its estimated cotton-suitable land despite having high potential. Cotton production in recent years has ranged roughly between 50,000–80,000 metric tons annually, with productivity commonly reported around 0.7–1.5 tons per hectare depending on irrigation, seed type, and management practices. Productivity in irrigated areas such as Afar and the Awash Basin is generally much higher than rain-fed systems. The government considers cotton a strategic crop because of its importance for the expanding textile and apparel sector, foreign exchange earnings, and employment generation. Recent efforts, including the introduction of improved and Bt cotton varieties, aim to improve productivity and quality.', 37, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('cowpea', 'croptype-201421200829', 'food-legume', 'Cowpea', NULL, 'Vigna ungulculata', 'MARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Cowpea.jpg', 'Cowpea has been cultivated for many centuries in the developing world and well adapted to the stressful growing condition of the tropics and has excellent nutritional qualities. It is a grain legume, which can be grown in relatively infertile sandy soils with a minimum annual rainfall of 200 mm. It is a fast growing, drought resistant crop, which also improves soil fertility by fixing atmospheric nitrogen. Cowpea grain typically contains 230-250 g/kg crude protein (CP) and 500-670 g/kg starch on a dry matter bases and cowpea forage, i.e. the crop residue after harvesting grain, 210 CP and 600 g digestible dry matter per Kg dry matter.
Cowpea is primarily used in the form of dry seed cooked as a pulse in a large variety of dishes. Green beans or cut green pods used as a vegetable are of secondary importance. In some areas of semi humid tropics the cowpea provides more than half the plant protein in human diets. Sometimes cowpea is also grown for forage and as a cover crop.', 8, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('cuccumber', 'croptype-248065014733', 'fruit-and-vegetables', 'Cuccumber', NULL, 'Cucumis sativus', NULL, 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/cuccumber.jpg', NULL, 1, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('date-palm', 'croptype-710600158551', 'fruit-and-vegetables', 'Date palm', NULL, 'Phoenix dactylifera L.', 'WARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Date palm.jpg', NULL, 2, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('dekoko', 'croptype-309960988062', 'food-legume', 'Dekoko', '{"am": "ደቀቆ"}'::jsonb, 'Pisum sativum var. abyssinicum', 'AlaARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/dekeko.jfif', 'Dekoko is a pulse crop cultivated and developed in Ethiopia. The crop is known to grow in few pocket areas especially in South Tigray and North Wello regions. Dekoko grows from mid-altitude areas to highland areas. Although it is a cool season crop like field pea, the crop has an ability to grow in the low land areas such as Chercher where the elevation is 1720 masl. In higher areas, however, Dekoko needs late sowing (from mid July to early August) and a soil free of water logging condition. As Dekoko is a crop that matures within three months of planting, it is usually weeded once or twice before it produces tenrils and flowers but latter stages it is difficult to differentiate the root with weeds and usually uprooted with weed plants. The crop grows best on different types of soils with well-drained.', 2, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('durum-wheat', 'croptype-303724598967', 'cereal', 'Durum wheat', '{"am": "የመኮሮኒ ስንዴ"}'::jsonb, 'Triticum turgidum var. durum', 'DZARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Durum wheat.jpg', 'Durum or macaroni wheat is indigenous to Ethiopia and it has been under cultivation since ancient times. Ethiopia is considered to be the center of genetic diversity of this crop. It is a tetraploid wheat species and traditionally grown on heavy black clay soil (Vertisols) of the central and northern highlands of Ethiopia between 1800-2800 meters above sea level. Accurate statistics on area and production of durum wheat in the county are difficult to obtain since they are lumped together with bread wheat.

In Ethiopia, durum wheat is consumed as leavened bread, common bread, macaroni, spaghetti, biscuits, pastries, and in various indigenous food preparations. The straw is mainly used for cattle feed and for fuel at times of scarcity. It has very narrow d=adaptation and lower yield potential as compared to bread wheat.

Although recommendations may vary from one region to another, the planting of newly improved durum wheat varieties are 2-3 weeks earlier than the normally used land races. In general, the improved varieties of durum wheat are highly beneficial in most of their qualities to agro industries.', 43, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('eggplant', 'croptype-058978345314', 'fruit-and-vegetables', 'Eggplant', NULL, 'Solannum melongena L.', NULL, 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/eggplant.jpg', NULL, 1, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('emmer-wheat', 'croptype-492103822592', 'cereal', 'Emmer wheat', '{"am": "አጃ"}'::jsonb, 'Triticum dicoccum', 'SinARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Emmer wheat.jpg', 'Emmer wheat (Triticum dicoccum) is not strictly African; it is originated in the Near East. Indeed, it is one of the first cereals ever domesticated and was part of the early agriculture of the Fertile Crescent.

Farmers have it in fields perhaps as far back as 10,000 years ago. For several thousand years it remained a major cereal throughout the Middle East and North Africa. Then people switched to durum wheat, the type now used worldwide to make spaghetti, macaroni and other pastas. Farmers preferred it because its grain was free threshing (the seed fell out of its husk quite easily), and during the past 2,000 years or so the older form, emmer wheat, became an abandoned wheat. At national level, during 2020/21 cropping season 15,502.18 ha of land is covered by Emmer (aja) wheat and about 305,403.43 quintals are produced in this cropping season.
Emmer, locally known as Aja, is used in various ways. Some is ground into flour and baked into special bread (kita). Some is crushed and cooked with milk or water to make porridge (Genfo). And some is mixed with boiling water and butter to produce gruel. With emmer''s high protein content and smooth and easily digestible starch, infants and nursing mothers especially favor the gruel.

Source: Crop Variety Register, IssueNo. 24. 2021. MoA', 3, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('enset', 'croptype-332505833002', 'roots-and-tubers', 'Enset', '{"am": "እንሰት"}'::jsonb, 'Ensete ventricosum (Welw.) Cheesman', 'AreARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/enset.jpg', 'Enset is commonly known as "false banana" for its close resemblances to the domesticated banana plant.', 6, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('ethiopian-mustard', 'croptype-565133287269', 'oil-seeds', 'Ethiopian mustard', NULL, 'Brassica carinata', 'HARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Ethiopian mustard.jpg', NULL, 10, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('faba-bean', 'croptype-720981978697', 'food-legume', 'Faba bean', NULL, 'Vicia faba', 'HARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Faba bean.jpg', 'Ethiopia is probably one of the primary centers of diversification for faba bean. Although the small seeded type of the Ethiopian faba bean is not well studied, there are some reports of tremendous diversity in protein content, chocolate spot and leaf rust resistance.
Faba bean is produced in many regions of Ethiopia. The major producing regions are Tigray, Gondar, Gojjam, Wollega, Wollo, Gamo Gofa and Shoa.', 40, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('fenugreek', 'croptype-537295686678', 'spices-condiments-medicinal-aromatic', 'Fenugreek', '{"am": "አብሽ"}'::jsonb, 'Trigonella foenum-graecum', 'SinARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/fenugreek.jfif', 'Fenugreek is a plant in the family of Fabaceae. It is used both as an herb (the leaves) and as a spice (the seed). It is cultivated worldwide to a semi-arid crops. It is frequently used in curry. The word for fenugreek is Amharic is abesh, and the seed is often used in Ethiopia as a natural herbal medicine in the treatment of diabetes. It is also sometimes used as an ingredient in the production of clarified butter, its flour is used as a flavoring of the traditional bread and maintains soft texture of "tef enjera".

Major fenugreek producing countries are India, Argentina, Egypt, France, Spain, Turkey, Morocco and China. Fenugreek seed is widely used as a galactagogue (milk producing agent) by nursing mothers to increase inadequate breast milk supply. Since the maple syrup-like flavor is strong and not always liked, the seeds are ground to a powder and administered in capsules. Studies have shown that fenugreek is a potent stimulator of breast milk production and its use was associated with increases in milk production of as much as 900%.
According to Central Statistics Agency 2020/21 report, the total area under production was 42,344.28 hectares and the production was estimated to be over 507,472.35 quintals.', 10, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('field-pea', 'croptype-346406364551', 'food-legume', 'Field pea', NULL, 'Pisum sativum', 'HARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Field pea.jpg', NULL, 46, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('fig', 'croptype-237310713191', 'fruit-and-vegetables', 'Fig', NULL, 'Ficus carica L.', 'MARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Fig.jpg', NULL, 2, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('fine-bush', NULL, 'fruit-and-vegetables', 'Fine bush', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/', NULL, 4, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('finger-millet', 'croptype-678775192009', 'cereal', 'Finger millet', '{"am": "ዳጉሳ"}'::jsonb, 'Eleusine coracana', 'MARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/finger_millet.webp', 'Finger millet, Amharic Dagusa also known as African millet, is an annual plant widely grown as a cereal in the arid areas of Africa and Asia. Finger millet is originally native to the Ethiopian Highlands and was introduced into India approximately 4000 years ago. It is very adaptable to higher elevations and is grown in the Himalaya up to 2,300 meters in elevation.

In Ethiopia, finger millet is produced in North Gonder, East Gojam, some parts of Tigray and West Wollega. The seed should be planted into a well-prepared seedbed, not deeper than 2-3 cm. Finger millet requires a well-distributed rainfall during growth, due to its expensive but shallow root system. Whilst moisture requirement is rather high, the crop should be grown on well drained soils with average annual rainfall above 800-900 mm.', 29, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('foxtail-millet', 'croptype-815519582877', 'cereal', 'Foxtail millet', NULL, 'Setaria italic', 'MARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Foxtail millet.jpg', 'It is the second most widely planted species of millet, and the most important in East Asia. It has the longest history of cultivation among the millets, having been grown in China since sometime in the sixth millennium BC. Other names for foxtail millet include Italian millet, German millet, Chinese millet, and Hungarian millet.

It is an annual grass with slim, vertical, leafy stems which can reach a height of 120-200 cm. The seed head is a dense, hairy panicle 5-30 cm long. The small seeds, around 2 mm in diameter, are encased in a thin, papery hull which is easily removed in threshing. Seed color varies greatly between varieties. It is a warm season crop, typically planted in late spring. Its early maturity and efficient use of available water make it suitable for rising in dry areas.

Nutrient composition is similar to common millet, approximately 11% of protein, 4% oil, and 6.7% of crude fiber. Foxtail millet grain has higher content of essential amino acids, vitamins (thiamine, riboflavin, niacin).', 2, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('garlic', 'croptype-142136063871', 'fruit-and-vegetables', 'Garlic', '{"am": "ነጭ ሽንኩርት"}'::jsonb, 'Allium sativum', 'DZARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/garlic.jpg', 'Garlic (Allium sativum L, 2n=16) belongs to the family Alliaceae and is the second most widely used Allium next to onion. In Ethiopia, small growers in the highlands grow garlic traditionally but due to faulty cultural practices, yields are generally low. The yield in large-scale production with irrigation is expected to be about 10tons per hectre. Garlic is adapted to cool climates and should not generally be planted at an altitude below 2000 masl. The amount of rainfall during the growing period (4.5 to 6 months) should be 600 mm to 700 mm. The optimum temperature for growing garlic lies between 12 and 24oC.
During 2020/21 Meher cropping season, the total area under production reaches 15,979.54 ha and the production is estimated to be 1,149,446.97 quintals', 9, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('geranium', 'croptype-423946881555', 'spices-condiments-medicinal-aromatic', 'Geranium', NULL, 'Pelargonium graveolens L. Herit', 'WGARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/geranium.webp', NULL, 1, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('ginger', 'croptype-459786504538', 'spices-condiments-medicinal-aromatic', 'Ginger', '{"am": "ዝንጅብል"}'::jsonb, 'Zingeber officinale Rose', 'TNSRC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/ginger.jfif', NULL, 2, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('grass-pea', 'croptype-114934769727', 'food-legume', 'Grass pea', NULL, 'Lathyrus sativus L.', 'DZARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Grass pea.jpg', 'Grass pea is among the important highland pulses of Ethiopia grown on more than 143,893.57 hectares with the production of about 2,899,174.75 quintals in 2020/21 cropping season. It is one of the important crops of economic significance in Ethiopia which is the fifth most important pulse crop after faba bean, field pea, chickpea and haricot bean. It is the cheapest source of protein in the diets of most people. Grass pea is a highly popular food and feed legume in the farming system duet to its tolerance of drought, flooding and diseases and also its importance in ameliorating soil fertility. It is commonly grown as a double crop after the cereals; tef or barley.', 1, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('green-courgette', 'croptype-646960654542', 'fruit-and-vegetables', 'Green courgette', NULL, 'Cucurbita spp', 'MARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/zuccuni.jpg', NULL, 1, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('groundnut', 'croptype-684407673538', 'oil-seeds', 'Groundnut', '{"am": "ኦቾሎኒ"}'::jsonb, 'Arachis hypogaea', 'WARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Groundnut.jpg', 'Ground is a species in the legume or "bean" family (Fabaceae). It is an annual herbaceous legume and a warm-season crop and need abundant sunshine and a warm climate for its normal growth. The most suitable soils are well-drained loose, friable, sandy loam, well supplied with lime and with moderate (but not high) amount of organic matter. Groundnut requires five months of warm weather. The pod ripen 120 to 150 days after the seeds are planted. If the crop is harvested too early, the pods will be unripe. If they are harvested late, the pods will snap off at the stalk, and will remain in the soil. Groundnut is particularly susceptible to contamination during growth and storage. Poor storage can lead to an infection by the mold fungus Aspergillus flavus, releasing the toxic and highly carcinogenic substance aflatoxin. The aflatoxin producing molds exist throughout the peanut growing areas and may produce aflatoxin in peanuts when conditions are favorable to fungal growth.', 32, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('haricot-bean', 'croptype-391765061369', 'food-legume', 'Haricot bean', NULL, 'Phaseolus vulgare', 'MARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Haricot bean.jpg', 'Haricot bean is one of the lowland pulse crops produced in the hot humid regions of Ethiopia. It has been known as an export crop for long period of time contributing to the foreign exchange earnings of the country.', 81, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('hibiscus', 'croptype-029136077947', 'spices-condiments-medicinal-aromatic', 'Hibiscus', '{"am": "ከርከዴ"}'::jsonb, 'Hibiscus subdariffa L.', 'WGARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Hibiscus.jpg', NULL, 2, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('japanese-mint', 'croptype-645048096801', 'spices-condiments-medicinal-aromatic', 'Japanese mint', '{"am": "ናና"}'::jsonb, 'Mentha arvensis L.', 'WGARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/Japanese mint.jpg', NULL, 1, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('kenaf', 'croptype-531239369740', 'industrial-crops', 'Kenaf', NULL, 'Hibiscus spp', 'WARC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/kenaf.webp', NULL, 1, 'ACTIVE'),
  ('kororima', 'croptype-747106721142', 'spices-condiments-medicinal-aromatic', 'Kororima', '{"am": "ኮረሪማ"}'::jsonb, 'Aframomum corrorima (Braun) PCM. Ja', 'TNSRC', 'http://196.191.93.34/moa-portal/cropopen/varieites/crops/korerima.jfif', NULL, 2, 'ACTIVE'),
